    +(C(\alpha))^2e^{-NT_0\alpha}\|\varphi\|_X^2\right],\\
    I_2:=4D(T_0)C(T_0)\int_{0}^{T_0}\|B^*(I-P_\alpha)S^*(t)S^*((N-1)T_0)\varphi\|_U^2
    \mathrm dt.
\end{cases}
\end{equation}
   According to Assumption $(A_3)$, $(iv)$ of Remark~\ref{yu-remark-8-00-1} and $(d_4)$, there exists  a constant $C(T_0,\alpha)>0$ so that
\begin{equation*}
    I_2\leq C(T_0,\alpha)e^{-(N-1)T_0\alpha}\|\varphi\|_X^2,
\end{equation*}
    which, combined with \eqref{yu-12-1-11}, \eqref{yu-12-1-12} and the fact $NT_0\leq T<(N+1)T_0$, indicates
\begin{equation*}\label{yu-12-1-14}
    \|S^*(T)\varphi\|_X^2
    \leq 4D(T_0)C(T_0)\int_0^T\|B^*S^*(t)\varphi\|_U^2\mathrm dt
    +e^{T_0\alpha}[2D(T_0)(C(\alpha))^2+C(T_0,\alpha)e^{\alpha T_0}]e^{-\alpha T}\|\varphi\|_X^2.
\end{equation*}
    Since $\varphi$, $\alpha$ and $T$ are arbitrary,  it follows from the latter estimate and
    \cite[Theorem 3.4]{Liu-Wang-Xu-Yu} that
    the pair $[A,B]$ is rapidly stabilizable.
\par
    In summary, we finish the proof of Theorem \ref{yu-theorem-9-01-1}.
\end{proof}

\section{Applications}\label{yu-sec-10-1-4}
In this section, we will provide two examples  as applications of Theorem \ref{yu-theorem-9-01-1}.
\subsection{Delayed heat equation with Neumann boundary control}  Let $\alpha>0$, $\tau>0$ and $\kappa\in\mathbb{R}$.
     Consider the following controlled equation:
\begin{equation}\label{m-25-7-21-1}
\begin{cases}
    y_t(t,x)=(\partial_x^2-\alpha ) y(t,x)+\kappa y(t-\tau,x),&(t,x)\in \mathbb{R}^+\times (0,1),\\
    y_x(t,0)=0,\;\;y_x(t,1)=u(t),&t\in \mathbb{R}^+,\\
    y(0,x)=y_0(x),&x\in(0,1),\\
    y(t,x)=\varphi(t,x),&(t,x)\in(-\tau,0)\times(0,1),
\end{cases}

