ref_dict.rk:Snapp:
\begin{remark}\label{rk:Snapp}
The augmented $K$-ring $K(M)$ of a matroid was introduced by Larson, Li, Payne, and Proudfoot \cite{LLPP}. Let $M$ be a matroid on ground set $E$ with subsets $S_1, \ldots, S_p$ such that the restriction polymatroid is $\sP$ (with cage $\bfm$). That is, 
\begin{enumerate}
\item the subsets $S_1, \ldots, S_p$ form a partition of $E$,
\item $|S_i| = m_i$ for all $i \in [p]$,
\item The rank function of the matroid, $\rk_M:2^E \to \N$ is preserved by the product of symmetric groups $\mathfrak{S}_{S_1}\times \cdots \times \mathfrak{S}_{S_p}$, and 
\item For each $J \subseteq [p]$, we have $$\rk_\sP(J) = \rk_M\left(\bigcup_{j \in J}S_j\right).$$
\end{enumerate}

Then, following the construction in \cite{LLPP}, the Snapper polynomial of $\sP$ is 
\[\mathrm{Snapp}_\sP(t_1,\ldots,t_p) \coloneqq \chi\left(M, \mathcal{L}_{S_1}^{\otimes t_1} \otimes \cdots \otimes \mathcal{L}_{S_p}^{\otimes t_p}\right),\]
where $\chi(M,-)\colon K(M) \to \Z$ denotes the Euler characteristic map and $\mathcal{L}_{S_i}$ denotes a line bundle. 

 It was also shown in \cite{CRMS} that $\mathrm{Snapp}_{\sP}(t_1,\ldots,t_p) = \mathfrak{b}(\cave_\sP(t_1,\ldots,t_p))$, where $\mathfrak{b}$ is the polynomial map sending monomials to their corresponding products of binomial expressions, that is, $$\mathfrak{b}\colon \Q[t_1,\ldots,t_p] \to \Q[t_1,\ldots,t_p] \ \text{ is given by } \ \mathfrak{b}\colon t_1^{n_1}\cdots t_p^{n_p} \mapsto \binom{t_1+n_1}{n_1}\cdots\binom{t_p+n_p}{n_p}.$$ Recall that Eur and Larson showed that $\mathrm{Snapp}_\sP(t_1,\ldots,t_p) = \mathfrak{b}(\mathrm{Box}_\sP(t_1,\ldots,t_p))$\cite[Proposition 2.8]{EL23}.
 \end{remark}

post_theorem_intro_text:
A certain class of polymatroids, called realizable polymatroids, are related to multiplicity-free varieties (in the sense of \cite{Brion}) in the following way: for any realizable polymatroid $\mathscr{P}$, one can construct a multiplicity-free variety $X_\mathscr{P} \subseteq \mathbb{P} \coloneqq \mathbb{P}^{m_1} \times \cdots \times \mathbb{P}^{m_p} $ whose multidegree support is $\mathscr{P}$ (see \cite[proof of Proposition 7.15]{CCRMM} for the construction). This construction is essential to prior understanding of the cave polynomial in the realizable case. We will give a brief summary of the proof of this result in the realizable case, and then in the arbitrary case. These proofs rely heavily on algebraic geometry. 

\subsection{The realizable case}\label{sec:realizable}

Let $\mathscr{P}$ be a polymatroid and $X_\mathscr{P}$ the multiplicity-free variety with $\mathscr{P}$ as its multidegree support. Then the coefficients of the three polynomials defined above appear in two places:  as the coefficients of the multigraded Hilbert polynomial of $X_\mathscr{P}$ written in terms of the binomial expressions $\binom{t_1+a_1}{a_1}\cdots\binom{t_p+a_p}{a_p}$, and  as the coefficients of the expression of $[X_\mathscr{P}]$ as a linear combination of the classes of $\mathcal{O}_{\mathbb{P}^{a_1} \times \cdots \times \mathbb{P}^{a_p}}$  in the Grothendieck ring $K(\mathbb{P})$. In \cite[Theorem 6.9]{CCRMM}, it was shown that the coefficients of the stalactite polynomial of $\mathscr{P}$ are also the Hilbert coefficients of the variety $X_\mathscr{P}$. The proof uses a shelling order of the simplicial complex $\Delta(J)$ associated to the generic initial ideal $J = \mathrm{gin}(\mathfrak{P})$ of the $\mathbb{N}^p$-graded prime ideal $\mathfrak{P}$ associated to $X_\mathscr{P}$. Conca and Tsakiris proved in \cite[Theorem 3.9]{ConcaTsakiris} that the multigraded Hilbert function is given by 
\[\sum_{\mathbf{n} \in I(\mathscr{P})}\prod_{i=1}^p\binom{t_i+n_i-1}{n_i},\]
i.e. that its coefficients are the same as those of the box polynomial. It was shown by Knutson in \cite[Theorem 3]{Knutson} that the coefficients describing the class of a multiplicity-free variety $X$ in the Grothendieck ring as a linear combination of Schubert classes are given by the M\"obius function of an order ideal in the Bruhat order. In \cite[Theorem 6.12(iii)]{CCRMM}, it was also proven that the Hilbert coefficients satisfy a M\"obius-like recurrence, i.e. that the stalactite polynomial is equal to the M\"obius polynomial. This proof uses properties of multiplicity-free varieties, namely their behavior under cuts by generic hyperplanes and projections.

\subsection{The arbitrary case}\label{sec:arbitrary}

Brion showed that any multiplicity-free variety $X_\mathscr{P}$ has a flat degeneration to a variety $Y_\mathscr{P}$ \cite{Brion}. In \cite[Definition 2.4]{CRMS} we call this the polymatroidal multiprojective variety of $\mathscr{P}$. For any polymatroid $\mathscr{P}$, we can define its dual polymatroidal ideal $J_\mathscr{P}$, and then $Y_\mathscr{P} = V(J_\mathscr{P})$. The generic initial ideal $J$ used in the proof that the stalactite coefficients are equal to the Hilbert coefficients coincides with $J_\mathscr{P}$, so this is true in the arbitrary case as well. It is known that the cave polynomial of a polymatroid is related to the Snapper polynomial of a polymatroid (see \autoref{rk:Snapp}). Eur and Larson gave the following formula for the Snapper polynomial in \cite[Proposition 2.8]{EL23}:
\[\mathrm{Snapp}_\mathscr{P}(t_1,\ldots,t_p) = \sum_{\mathbf{n} \in I(\mathscr{P})} \prod_{i=1}^p \binom{t_i+n_i-1}{n_i}.\]
This shows that the Box polynomial is equal to the cave polynomial in the arbitrary case. The flat degeneration of $X_\mathscr{P}$ to $Y_\mathscr{P}$ also means that we can use $Y_\mathscr{P}$ to compute the Hilbert polynomial, so the fact that the Hilbert coefficients are given by a M\"obius function is still true in the arbitrary case. \\

\subsection{Combinatorial Approach} The primary goal of this paper is to further combinatorialize the proof of \autoref{thm:equality}. In particular, we give an explicit formula for the M\"obius function and show that it computes the coefficients of the formula given in ~\autoref{def:box}. 

\begin{theorem}\label{thm:mob value} Let $\mathbf{m} \le \mathbf{n} < \hat{1}$ in $P$. Then  \[\mu_P(\mathbf{m},\mathbf{n}) = \begin{cases}
    (-1)^j & \text{if } \mathbf{m} = \mathbf{n}-\bfe_{i_1}-\cdots - \bfe_{i_j} \text{ for some distinct } i_1, \ldots, i_j \in [p],\\
    0 & \text{otherwise.}
\end{cases}\]
\end{theorem}

We also show that the number of stalactites containing a point is given by a M\"obius-like recurrence. 

\begin{theorem} \label{thm:stal mob}
    For each $\mathbf{n} \in \mathbb{N}^p$, the number of stalactites $c_\mathbf{n}(\mathscr{P})$ obeys the following recurrence: 
    $$c_\mathbf{n}(\mathscr{P}) = \begin{cases}
    1 & \text{if } \mathbf{n} \in B(\mathscr{P}),\\
1-\displaystyle\sum_{\mathbf{m} >\mathbf{n}} c_\mathbf{m}(\mathscr{P}) &\text{if } \mathbf{n} \in I(\mathscr{P}) \setminus B(\mathscr{P}),\\
0 & \text{if }\mathbf{n} \notin I(\mathscr{P}). 
    \end{cases}
    $$

\noindent    That is, $c_\mathbf{n}(\mathscr{P}) = \mu_\mathscr{P}(\mathbf{n}).$
\end{theorem}

One application of these results is to the Snapper polynomial. 

\begin{remark}\label{rk:Snapp}
The augmented $K$-ring $K(M)$ of a matroid was introduced by Larson, Li, Payne, and Proudfoot \cite{LLPP}. Let $M$ be a matroid on ground set $E$ with subsets $S_1, \ldots, S_p$ such that the restriction polymatroid is $\mathscr{P}$ (with cage $\mathbf{m}$). That is, 
\begin{enumerate}
\item the subsets $S_1, \ldots, S_p$ form a partition of $E$,
\item $|S_i| = m_i$ for all $i \in [p]$,
\item The rank function of the matroid, $\rk_M:2^E \to \mathbb{N}$ is preserved by the product of symmetric groups $\mathfrak{S}_{S_1}\times \cdots \times \mathfrak{S}_{S_p}$, and 
\item For each $J \subseteq [p]$, we have $$\rk_\mathscr{P}(J) = \rk_M\left(\bigcup_{j \in J}S_j\right).$$
\end{enumerate}

Then, following the construction in \cite{LLPP}, the Snapper polynomial of $\mathscr{P}$ is 
\[\mathrm{Snapp}_\mathscr{P}(t_1,\ldots,t_p) \coloneqq \chi\left(M, \mathcal{L}_{S_1}^{\otimes t_1} \otimes \cdots \otimes \mathcal{L}_{S_p}^{\otimes t_p}\right),\]
where $\chi(M,-)\colon K(M) \to \mathbb{Z}$ denotes the Euler characteristic map and $\mathcal{L}_{S_i}$ denotes a line bundle. 

 It was also shown in \cite{CRMS} that $\mathrm{Snapp}_{\mathscr{P}}(t_1,\ldots,t_p) = \mathfrak{b}(\cave_\mathscr{P}(t_1,\ldots,t_p))$, where $\mathfrak{b}$ is the polynomial map sending monomials to their corresponding products of binomial expressions, that is, $$\mathfrak{b}\colon \mathbb{Q}[t_1,\ldots,t_p] \to \mathbb{Q}[t_1,\ldots,t_p] \ \text{ is given by } \ \mathfrak{b}\colon t_1^{n_1}\cdots t_p^{n_p} \mapsto \binom{t_1+n_1}{n_1}\cdots\binom{t_p+n_p}{n_p}.$$ Recall that Eur and Larson showed that $\mathrm{Snapp}_\mathscr{P}(t_1,\ldots,t_p) = \mathfrak{b}(\mathrm{Box}_\mathscr{P}(t_1,\ldots,t_p))$\cite[Proposition 2.8]{EL23}.
 \end{remark}

 From \autoref{thm:equality}, we get the following equalities. 

\begin{corollary} Let $\mathfrak{b}$ be the map defined above. We have

\[\mathrm{Snapp}_{\mathscr{P}}(t_1,\ldots,t_p) 
    = \mathfrak{b}(\textnormal{M\"ob}_\mathscr{P}(t_1,\ldots,t_p)) 
    = \mathfrak{b}(\Stal_\mathscr{P}(t_1,\ldots,t_p)).\]
\end{corollary}

