We may reinterpret the following theorem as a result about the convergence of the Taylor expansion for certain spaces of holomorphic functions, using that the Bargmann transform $\mathcal{B}$ is an isometric isomorphism between $M^p(\R)$ and the Fock spaces $\mathcal{F}^p(\C)$ and that $\mathcal{B}h_n(z)=\left(\frac{\pi}{n!}\right)^{n/2}z^n$, so the subsequent corollary about Taylor polynomials “follows directly” from it.

\begin{theorem}
\label{thm:hermiteins0}
    Let $1\leq p\leq \infty$. There exists a  constant $C=C_{p}$ such that for every $N\in\N$ 
    \begin{equation}\label{eq:conv-Mp}
        \big\|S_Nf\big\|_{M^p(\R)}\leq C\|f\|_{M^p(\R)},\qquad f\in M^p(\R),
    \end{equation}  
    if and only if $1<p<\infty$.
For $p=1$, there exist   constants $c,C>1$ such that 
\begin{equation}\label{eq:non-unif}
   \big\|S_N  \big\|_{M^1(\R)\to M^1(\R)}\ge C\log(c N) ,
\end{equation}
In particular, 
\begin{enumerate}
    \item for every $f\in M^p(\R),\ 1<p<\infty$, $\lim_{N\to \infty}S_Nf= f$
    in $M^p(\R^d)$
\item there exists $f\in M^1(\R) $ such that 
    $\|S_Nf\|_{M^2(\R)}\to\infty$ so that $S_Nf$ does not converge to $f$ in $M^1(\R)$.
    \end{enumerate}
\end{theorem}

Beyond this equivalence, the paper “provide[s] an alternative proof that is more direct and uses only elementary tools.” For the range $1<p<\infty$, the proof of the estimate above “relies on \cite[Proposition~4.3]{multipliers} where a connection between boundedness of Hermite multipliers in $M^p(\R^d)$ and Fourier multipliers on $L^p(\T^d)$ was shown”; this “also allows [them] to study Bochner-Riesz means of Hermite series.” Finally, the result “extends in a straightforward way” to higher dimensions via tensor products of Hermite functions.

