The post-theorem text gives the following informal description tied to the main proposition: the infimum in
\begin{equation}\label{eq: benamou}
        W_p(f(0),f(1)) = \inf_{(f_t,v_t)} \left(\int_0^1 \int ||v_t||^p df_t dt \right)^{\frac{1}{p}}
    \end{equation}
"is achieved by choosing $f_t$ as a constant speed $W_p$ geodesic and $v_t$ satisfying $\|v_t\| = |\dot f|_{W_p}$" (with constant speed geodesics defined by $d(f_s,f_t)=|s-t|d(f_0,f_1)$ and speed $|\dot f|_d=\lim_{h\to0}\frac1h d(f_{t+h},f_t)$). On $\mathbb{R}^d$ these constant-speed $W_p$ geodesics are identified as "the McCann displacement interpolations". The text then explains why this approach does not directly extend to discrete domains: for measures on $\{0,1,\dots,n\}\subset\mathbb R$, McCann interpolations "will traverse $\mathbb R$, but not remain in the domain," so "considering them as embedded in a continuum will not suffice" and this "sacrifices the classical Benamou-Brenier formula."

expanded_theorem:
[Benamou-Brenier Formulation]\label{prop: benamou}
    Given two distributions $f(0),f(1) \in \mathcal{P}(\mathbb{R}^d)$, we can then express
    \begin{equation}\label{eq: benamou}
        W_p(f(0),f(1)) = \inf_{(f_t,v_t)} \left(\int_0^1 \int ||v_t||^p df_t dt \right)^{\frac{1}{p}}
    \end{equation}
     where $(f_t,v_t)$ are constrained by the transport equation - also known as the continuity equation:
    \begin{equation}\label{eq: continuity}
        \partial_t f_t + \nabla \cdot (f_t v_t) = 0
    \end{equation}
    where $\nabla \cdot$ is the divergence operator, (see \cite{benamou2000computational}).,

